.el .ds Aq '
.TH ifg-mcp 1  "ifg-mcp 0.9.0"
.SH NAME
ifg\-mcp \- Print an MCP client config for IronForge\*(Aqs stdio MCP server
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBifg mcp\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print an MCP client config for IronForge\*(Aqs stdio MCP server
.PP
Prints a JSON snippet (`mcpServers`) that starts `ironforge\-mcp` with the resolved host. The token is never printed: the snippet refers to `${IRONFORGE_PAT}`, which you export in the environment the client starts from (for example `export IRONFORGE_PAT="$(ifg auth token)"`). Nothing is written to disk and no request is made to the server.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
ifg\-mcp\-config(1)
Print an MCP client config snippet for the resolved host (stdout)
.TP
ifg\-mcp\-doctor(1)
Check whether the MCP server can be used from this machine
.TP
ifg\-mcp\-help(1)
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)
.SH VERSION
v0.9.0
